, summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"3db5b699-964c-444d-a54a-6b8a029919b5","name":"Guruvayur Sri Krishna Temple","deity":"Krishna","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Thrissur","town":"Guruvayur","latitude":10.5949,"longitude":76.0405,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"no","samidhadhanam_notes":"No known space for Samidhadhanam within or near the temple premises.","food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Guruvayur Devaswom Oottupura (dining hall) — free meals served to pilgrims","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0.2,"nearest_river_name":"Karuvannur River","river_distance_km":6.8,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb is cooler; avoid Ekadashi day if you need a quick darshan, as crowds swell significantly.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The image is said to have been worshipped by Krishna himself at Dwaraka, and to have been carried at the time of the submersion of that city by Brihaspati (Guru) and Vayu, who installed it here — giving the place its name, Guruvayur.","etiquette_notes":"Strict traditional dress code, enforced at the entrance: men must be bare-chested, wearing only a mundu/dhoti (no shirt, no trousers); women wear a saree or a churidar/long skirt with blouse — no salwar-kameez tops alone, no western clothing. Only Hindus are permitted inside; the rule is enforced by temple staff.","native_name":"ഗുരുവായൂർ ശ്രീകൃഷ്ണ ക്ഷേത്രം","morning_opens_at":"03:00:00","morning_closes_at":"13:00:00","evening_opens_at":"15:00:00","evening_closes_at":"21:00:00","timings_notes":"One of the most consistent daily schedules of any major temple, but special poojas and festival days do shift it — confirm before travelling.","accessibility_no