jnas; Vishnu slew Kesan and used Kesi as a pillow, and is known here as Adhikesava for the killing of Kesan. Inscriptions date the site to 779 in the Kollam Era.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"ஆதிகேசவ பெருமாள் கோயில்","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Thiruvananthapuram International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":41.7,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Kuzhiturai railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":6.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition; temple inscriptions","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"44089e32-485c-4413-a061-2ab3edc17037","name":"Chandika Sthan","deity":"Chandika (Sati)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Bihar","district":"Munger","town":"Munger","latitude":25.39244,"longitude":86.48092,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Garkhe River","river_distance_km":2.3,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:59.639499+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple about two kilometres from Munger town, counted among the fifty-one Shakta pithas and regarded as a Siddhi-Peetha. According to tradition, Sati's left eye fell here, and worshippers are believed to be relieved of eye ailments. It is a major pilgrimage centre of the Ang region of Bihar.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Purnea Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":101.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Munger railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Chandika Sthan), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"4421298d-172c-448d-a4b5-939e4dadc3f3","name":"Kadachira Sri Thrikkapalam Siva Temple","deity":"Shiva as Dakshinamurthy","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Kannur","town":"Kadachira","latitude":11.8339,"longitude":75.4595,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Anjarakandi","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:03:52.882408+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"An ancient Shiva temple on the Anjarakandi river in Kannur district, where Shiva is worshipped as Dakshinamurthy, the teacher aspect. Two equally important Dakshinamurthy idols stand in separate east-facing sanctums. According to folklore, Parasurama installed the idol, and it is among Kerala's 108 Shiva temples.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Kannur International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":13.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Etakkot railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":3.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Kadachira Sri Thrikkapalam Siva Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"4421c960-b620-4823-9f2e-5cb22fa84903","name":"Vellayani Devi Temple","deity":"Bhadrakali","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Thiruvananthapuram","town":"Thiruvananthapuram","latitude":8.44556,"longitude":76.99139,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Vellayani Lake","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:59.708182+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:48.914317+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Bhadrakali temple on the eastern bank of Vellayani Lake, about 12 km southeast of Thiruvananthapuram, managed by the Travancore Devaswom Board. It has a bronze-roofed sanctum in Dravidian style and eastern and northern gopurams. It is noted for a festival of roughly 65 to 70 days held every three years, described as South India's longest continuous temple festival.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Thiruvananthapuram International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":8.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Nemom railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":2.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Vellayani Devi Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Dravidian","construction_century":null},{"id":"4431ef30-c0d0-40ba-b9df-c5744aaef46a","name":"Padmavati Temple","deity":"Padmavati (Alamelu Mangamma), consort of Venkateswara","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Andhra Pradesh","district":"Tirupati","town":"Daminedu","latitude":13.60781,"longitude":79.45011,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"sami